"""Class 10 SST Ch 15 Gender, Religion and Caste - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"Gender division in society is not based on biology but on social expectations and stereotypes.", r"Most women's household work, such as cooking, cleaning and looking after children, is unpaid and is seen as 'natural' to them.", "a",
     r"The sexual division of labour assigns domestic work to women through social stereotypes rather than biology; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Women's representation in the Lok Sabha and state assemblies in India has been low.", r"The Panchayati Raj system in India reserves one-third of seats for women in local bodies.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but the reservation in local bodies does not explain the low representation in the legislatures."),
 AR4(r"The Equal Wages Act of 1976 provides that equal wages should be paid for equal work.", r"The Act applies only to women working in factories.", "c",
     r"A is true. R is false: the Act applies to work of equal value and prohibits discrimination between men and women in wages."),
 AR4(r"India is a secular state because it has an official state religion.", r"The Constitution of India does not give any special status to any religion.", "d",
     r"A is false: India has no official religion. R is true and in fact explains why India is a secular state."),
 AR4(r"Communalism leads to conflict between religious groups.", r"Communalism is based on the idea that religion is the principal basis of social community.", "a",
     r"If religion is made the only basis of community, followers of different religions cannot live as equals, so conflict follows; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Caste is the only factor which decides how people vote in elections in India.", r"Voters also take into account the party, the candidate's performance and many other issues, and no party wins only on the votes of one caste.", "d",
     r"A is false because caste is one factor among many; R is true."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In Sunita's village the women of every family wake up early, cook, clean, fetch water and look after children before going out to work in the fields or shops. The men then do their paid work. When Sunita's brother joined a job, he got Rs 600 a day and when Sunita did the same work she got Rs 500. "
   r"(i) What is meant by the sexual division of labour? (ii) What is the main reason for women's work being unpaid? (iii) Which Act provides for equal wages for equal work? (iv) Why is the work done by women at home not counted as work in the economy?",
   r"(i) A system in which all work inside the home is done by women and work outside by men (1). (ii) It is considered a 'natural' duty of women (1). (iii) The Equal Wages Act (Equal Remuneration Act) (1). (iv) It is not paid and has no market value, so it is not counted in income or the economy even though it is essential (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A communal party wants to win votes by appealing to people of one religion. Its leaders say that followers of other faiths cannot be part of the same nation. In another town, communal riots break out, and families suffer. The state, however, does not support any religion and treats all as equal. "
   r"(i) What is communalism? (ii) What kind of state does not give a special status to any religion? (iii) Name any one form that communalism can take in politics. (iv) Why is communalism a threat to democracy?",
   r"(i) The belief that religion is the principal basis of social community, so people of one religion form one community and those of others cannot (1). (ii) A secular state (1). (iii) Any one: religious prejudice, majoritarian domination, communal political mobilisation or violence (1). (iv) It divides people, seeks to dominate minorities and, at its worst, causes violence, which goes against equality and fraternity in a democracy (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Before elections, a party picks candidates for a constituency by looking at the caste composition of voters. Another party appeals to the people of 'backward' castes. Yet in some elections the voters rejected a candidate of their own caste and voted for another on the basis of performance. "
   r"(i) Name one social reformer who fought against caste inequality. (ii) What is meant by caste politics? (iii) Does caste alone decide election results? (iv) How has caste politics given new voice to the disadvantaged castes?",
   r"(i) Any one: Jyotirao Phule, B. R. Ambedkar, Mahatma Gandhi or Periyar (1). (ii) Use of caste identity to gain political power or votes (1). (iii) No (1). (iv) It made disadvantaged castes demand a share in power and decision-making, and brought them into politics, so some groups have gained access to office and education (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

